(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-13-summarizing-the-evidence-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 13: Summarizing the Evidence Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A systematic review of observational studies of antioxidant vitamins to prevent cardiovascular disease combined the results of 12 studies to obtain a summary effect size and confidence interval. Which of the following would be the strongest rationale for combining study results?","choices":["You wish to obtain a more generalizable conclusion.","A statistical test shows that the studies are heterogeneous.","Most of the component studies are statistically significant.","The component studies have different, and to some extent complementary, biases.","You can obtain a more precise estimate of effect size."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Meta-analysis weights and pools the numerical results of similar studies, so the larger combined sample gives a stronger, more precise estimate of effect size (a narrower confidence interval) than any single study can. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.1"},{"q":"You are asked to critique a review of the literature on whether alcohol is a risk factor for breast cancer. The reviewer has searched MEDLINE and found several observational studies of this question but has not searched elsewhere. All of the following are limitations of this search strategy except:","choices":["Studies with negative results tend to not be published.","MEDLINE searches typically miss some articles, even those included in the database.","MEDLINE does not include all of the world's journals.","MEDLINE can be simultaneously searched for both content area and methods."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, this is the exception. Being able to search MEDLINE for both the content area and study methods at once is a strength that helps narrow a search, not a limitation. The limitations are missed articles, incomplete journal coverage, and publication bias. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.2"},{"q":"A systematic review of antiplatelet drugs for cardiovascular disease prevention combined individual patients, not trials. Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It allows for ecological comparisons.","It is more efficient for the investigator.","Subgroup analyses are possible.","It is not necessary to choose between fixed and random effects models when combining data.","Publication bias is less likely."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Individual trials are usually too small to examine subgroups, but pooling patients from several trials gives enough people in each subgroup to compare effects, and meta-analysis is noted for detecting differences in subgroups. This works only when patients, not trials, are pooled. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is not generally used to define a specific clinical question studied by randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Covariates that were taken into account","Interventions (e.g., exposure or experimental treatment)","Comparison group (e.g., patients taking placebo in a randomized trial)","Outcomes","Patients in the trials"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A specific clinical question is defined by PICO: patients, intervention, comparison, and outcomes. Covariates are not part of the question; they bear on how well the question can be answered, such as by confounding or effect modification. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.4"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes ways of measuring study quality?","choices":["The validity of summary measures of study quality is well established.","A description of study quality is a useful part of systematic reviews.","In a summary measure of quality, strengths of the study can make up for weaknesses.","Individual measures of quality such as randomization and blinding are not associated with study results."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A good systematic review describes each included study's characteristics and risk of bias, so readers can judge how strong the conclusions are. Assessing the quality of the included studies is a standard step in evaluating a review. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.5"},{"q":"Which of the following kinds of studies is least likely to be published?","choices":["Small positive studies","Large negative studies","Large positive studies","Small negative studies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Research without statistically significant results is less likely to be published, and small studies do not carry enough weight to overcome this, whereas large studies are usually published regardless of their findings. This is the core of publication bias. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is a comparative advantage of traditional (“narrative”) reviews over systematic reviews?","choices":["Readers can confirm that evidence cited is selected without bias.","Narrative reviews can review a broad range of questions bearing on the care of a condition.","They rely on the experience and judgment of an expert in the field.","They provide a quantitative summary of effects.","The scientific strength of the studies cited is explicitly evaluated."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Narrative reviews can address broad topics and a range of issues, such as the whole set of questions involved in caring for a condition. The cost is an unstructured approach with no transparent description of how the evidence was chosen. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.7"},{"q":"Which of the following is not always part of a typical forest plot?","choices":["The number of studies that meet inclusion criteria for quality","A summary or pooled effect size with confidence interval","Point estimates of effect size for each study","Confidence intervals for each study","The size or weight contributed by each study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pooled (summary) effect with its confidence interval, shown as a diamond, is included only if pooling is justified. If the studies are too different, the plot shows the individual studies without combining them. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.8"},{"q":"Which of the following cannot be used for identifying severity of illness as a reason for heterogeneity in a systematic review with meta-analysis?","choices":["Controlling for severity of illness in a study-level meta-analysis","A mathematical model relating average severity of illness to outcome across the component studies","A comparison of summary effect sizes in studies stratified by mean severity of illness","Subgroup analyses of patient-level data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a study-level meta-analysis only summary data from each study are available, so you cannot control for a covariate such as severity of illness as could be done in a single study or in a patient-level analysis. You can only compare studies with different average severities. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.9"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best justification for combining the results of five studies into a single summary effect?","choices":["A statistical test does not detect heterogeneity.","All studies are of high quality.","The patients, interventions, and outcomes are relatively similar.","Publication bias has been ruled out by a funnel plot.","The random effects model will be used to calculate the summary effect size."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Studies must be similar in order to be pooled, and this is judged by informed review of the patients, interventions, and outcomes rather than by a statistic alone. Combining dissimilar studies can be misleading. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.10"},{"q":"Which of the following is most correct for network meta-analyses?","choices":["Multiple interventions are directly compared to each other and ranked.","They require individual-level patient data for the analysis.","They require randomized controlled trials for analysis.","They do not require similar interventions for analysis.","They show the additional effects of new studies on the summary estimate."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The interventions in a network meta-analysis can be diverse. What matters is that the studies address similar patients and similar outcomes, which allows both direct and indirect comparisons among the interventions or tests. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.11"},{"q":"Which of the following is an advantage of the random effects model over the fixed effect model?","choices":["It can be used for studies with time-to-event analyses.","It describes the summary effect size for a single, narrowly defined question.","It is better suited for meta-analyses of diagnostic test performance.","Confidence intervals tend to be narrower.","It gives more realistic results when there is heterogeneity among studies."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The random effects model allows for variation among studies that is not due to chance, and so is used when heterogeneity is present. It gives wider confidence intervals that are more likely to be accurate than the narrower ones a fixed effect model would give. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.12"}]};
